(version 1)

# nice!view reversible display mount
#
# Structure mirrors the battery holes: the FRONT footprint owns the hole and
# carries the F.Cu pad; the BACK footprint carries only its B.Cu pad, centred on
# the same hole. One barrel per site, drilled once.
#
# The barrel is NON-PLATED, and that is what makes the mount reversible. The
# front footprint sits at rot 99 and the back at rot -81, so one physical hole
# is MOSI when the display mounts front and CS when it mounts back:
#
#   (102.101, 68.435)  front pad 1 MOSI  /  back pad 5 CS
#   (101.704, 65.926)  front pad 2 SCK   /  back pad 4 GND
#   (101.307, 63.417)  front pad 3 VCC   /  back pad 3 VCC     (self-symmetric)
#   (100.909, 60.909)  front pad 4 GND   /  back pad 2 SCK
#   (100.512, 58.400)  front pad 5 CS    /  back pad 1 MOSI
#
# A plated barrel would short each of those pairs together. Unplated keeps the
# two faces independent -- verified by test on the battery holes, same geometry.
#
# Assembly: everything (XIAO, display, JST, switches, diodes) mounts on ONE
# side and that side's solder jumpers are bridged. The display's pins are then
# soldered from the OPPOSITE face, which is why the far-face pad must net to
# the near-face jumpers.
#
# KiCad reports copper concentric with an NPTH as a hole-clearance error. Here
# the concentricity IS the design. Waived for this footprint only; the condition
# names both A and B so it also covers a routed track arriving at a pad.
# Verified: removing this rule returns 10 hole_clearance errors, and a
# non-existent reference in the same condition waives nothing.
#
# The matching solder-mask exception is NOT here -- it lives on the footprint
# itself as (attr ... allow_soldermask_bridges), which is tighter than a rule.

(rule "niceview_npth_ring_hole_clearance"
	(constraint hole_clearance (min 0mm))
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('display*') || B.memberOfFootprint('display*')"))

# Same exemption for the TRACK that arrives at a display ring. memberOfFootprint
# covers pads; a track entering the ring is a free item and is tested against
# the barrel by net. Scope by the hole nets so no other NPTH is affected.
(rule "niceview_npth_track_to_ring"
	(constraint hole_clearance (min 0mm))
	(condition "(A.Type == 'Track' && (A.NetName == 'DISP_H1' || A.NetName == 'DISP_H2' || A.NetName == 'DISP_H4' || A.NetName == 'DISP_H5' || A.NetName == 'VCC') && B.memberOfFootprint('display*')) || (B.Type == 'Track' && (B.NetName == 'DISP_H1' || B.NetName == 'DISP_H2' || B.NetName == 'DISP_H4' || B.NetName == 'DISP_H5' || B.NetName == 'VCC') && A.memberOfFootprint('display*'))"))

# Per-switch diode nests INSIDE the switch courtyard
#
# The PG1316S has a pocket in its body for the LED/diode. Chalk puts the SOD-123
# there at switch-local (0, -2.700) -- measured across all 21 of its diodes,
# stdev 0.0000 -- and the datasheet LAND PATTERN RECOMMENDATION shows the pocket
# on the opposite half of the body from the two terminals (ours at y = +2.650).
#
# So the diode courtyard is SUPPOSED to sit inside the switch courtyard. KiCad
# calls that 42 courtyard overlaps; here it is the intended assembly. The diode
# lies under the switch body, not beside it.
#
# Scoped to diode references only. Verified: the same rule with a non-existent
# reference ('NOPE*') waives nothing (42 overlaps remain), so this cannot leak
# board-wide and silently hide a real collision between other parts.

(rule "diode_nests_inside_switch_courtyard"
	(constraint courtyard_clearance (min -2mm))
	(condition "A.Reference == 'D*' || B.Reference == 'D*'"))


# Battery holes: unplated barrel, one pad per face, INDEPENDENT nets
#
# Mirroring the XIAO swaps pads 28 and 29, so each physical site is BAT+ for one
# mount side and BAT- for the other:
#
#     site A (128.494, 115.982) = front pad 28 / back pad 29
#     site B (126.494, 115.982) = front pad 29 / back pad 28
#
# The barrel is therefore NON-PLATED. A plated barrel would tie the two faces
# together, which ties BAT+ to BAT- through the board -- a dead short across the
# cell. Same reasoning as the nice!view display holes, and the same structure:
# a bare unnumbered np_thru_hole for the barrel plus separate SMD pads carrying
# the copper, because an NPTH pad cannot hold a net.
#
# The hole belongs to the FRONT footprint only. Both nice_view footprints emit
# coincident NPTH and each display hole is consequently drilled twice; giving
# the barrel a single owner avoids repeating that here.
#
# Verified by test: netting front pad 28 BATT_POS and back pad 29 BATT_NEG and
# routing a track to each on its own face gives 0 unconnected items and no
# short -- the faces really are independent.
#
# KiCad flags copper concentric with an NPTH by design. Waived below, scoped to
# the XIAO (REF**) and to pad numbers 28/29 only, plus tracks arriving at them.

(rule "xiao_battery_npth_concentric_copper"
	(constraint hole_clearance (min 0mm))
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('REF**') || B.memberOfFootprint('REF**')"))

(rule "xiao_battery_npth_track_to_ring"
	(constraint hole_clearance (min 0mm))
	(condition "(A.Type == 'Track' && (A.NetName == 'BATH_A' || A.NetName == 'BATH_B') && B.memberOfFootprint('REF**')) || (B.Type == 'Track' && (B.NetName == 'BATH_A' || B.NetName == 'BATH_B') && A.memberOfFootprint('REF**'))"))

# NOTE: the 2 remaining npth_inside_courtyard reports are the battery barrels
# sitting under the opposite XIAO's courtyard -- unavoidable, since the two
# footprints are mirror images of each other and the hole has one owner. No
# waiver here on purpose: that check is not driven by any .kicad_dru constraint
# (courtyard_clearance does not suppress it -- verified, count stays 2 with
# three different conditions), so a rule would be dead code. Set the severity in
# Board Setup if you want it silenced.
